g it could ultimately be the need  for effective ways to combat covet 19  that gives a boost to paula castillo's  work  and now science update the covet studies  making the news this week long coveted  risks after vaccination and how stress  impacts mortality  some scientific studies have titles that  are really hard to decipher  but this one is pretty self-explanatory  you know straight away that it's about  long covet and whether it can affect  anyone even someone who's vaccinated  one of the most important takeaways is  that the researchers found that  vaccination does lower your chances of  getting long-covered if you end up  catching the disease despite being  vaccinated a breakthrough infection  it also lowers your risk of dying from  the disease  here's a breakdown of some of their  results  first how probable is it that someone  with a breakthrough infection will end  up later with long-covet symptoms  to answer that question the scientists  compared a group of 33 000 breakthrough  patients with a range of different  control groups in total data from around  13 million patients flowed into the  study so it was quite a large one one of  the authors interpreted the article's  long covet results in a twitter thread  compared to unvaccinated people who got  covet 19 he said vaccinated people with  breakthrough infections were 34 percent  less likely to die and 15 percent less  likely to develop long-covered symptoms  so in summary the study helps clarify an  issue that's long been under debate it  indicates that although vaccination does  indeed lower the risk of developing long  covet it does so less than experts had  hoped  it's not a silver bullet against the  condition  [Music]  stress is bad for you everyone knows  that right the authors of this new study  however are a lot more specific they say  they've shown it's bad for the immune  system and can cause diseases like covet  19 to be more severe and potentially  deadly  the researchers looked at the  distribution of immune system cells  called leukocytes and how they change in  situations of acute stress and how that  process is tailored and guided by  distinct regions in the brain  also known as white blood cells  leukocytes help defend the body against  pathogens like saskov2  by the way the study has so far been  published under what's known as  accelerated article preview so it hasn't  yet completed the full editing process  and another caveat the study was also in  mice  that's important to know because it  means we can't be certain the same  things happen in the same ways in humans  even so though the results are  interesting  they reveal that levels of certain white  blood cells fell when the animals were  exposed to acute stress  exactly the cells that are able to  recognize and neutralize specific  pathogens  then the researchers exposed the mice to  the saskov2 virus  and they observed that compared to the  relaxed peers in a control group the  stressed out animals had higher viral  loads and died at higher rates from the  disease  in other words in mice at least stress  seems to play a role in both covet 19  severity and progression  despite progress made in the field of  covid research the virus has resulted in  large numbers of deaths worldwide  so far the virus has claimed over 6  million lives  in london  those who have lost friends and  relatives have organized a memorial site  a place to commemorate those who lost  their fight against the virus  [Music]  one quiet act of love  a collective cry of grief and anger  each heart marking a life in the uk lost  to covet 19  inscribed by friends and loved ones left  behind  the first heart i painted was this one  here  which has got steve's naming so steve  was my partner for a long time and my  husband for three weeks  fran hall is among a group of bereaved  volunteers who helped create and  preserve london's covered memorial wall  it's now grown to over 150 000 hearts  spanning a staggering 500 meters  it's just flabbergasting seeing this  wall now and how far it goes it just  puts it into perspective it allows me to  remember my partner  and and make sure that it's almost  remembered  we'll never forget these people and  obviously no one has  so i really appreciate seeing this  but behind this official looking  memorial lies a remarkable story it's a  renegade project created without  permission  and on the day the first hearts were  painted fran was risking not only a fine  but jail i've never broken the lawn  knowingly in my life so you know there  was a feeling of like oh my goodness i  hope i don't get arrested but actually  at the same time those feeling i  actually don't care i don't care the  worst thing in the world has happened to  me  and i don't care i don't care about  anything like that anymore behind the  scenes they were being advised by the  anti-government activist group known as  led by donkeys who helped set them up  with signs and sandwich boards to appear  as official as possible  over a year later the wall still stands  but it's not without its controversy  deliberately set opposite parliament  it's making a political statement  seeking to criticize the government's  handling of the pandemic  but should a memorial which aims to  represent all promote the political  opinions of some  i think the beauty of the wall is that  it can be whatever you want it to be if  you just want the wall as a place of  remembrance then that is what the wall  can be used for  if actually you feel angry at the  government putting a heart on the wall  is a is an act of almost an act of  protest but it doesn't have to be all  those things the government says it's  planning to establish an official  commemoration  but to creators like tom the grassroots  origins of this memorial are at the  heart of its power we felt really  strongly it was right that this act of  memorialization was was not in the hands  of the government this wall is a is a  people's wall it's a wall for the people  who lost loved ones that i think is the  beauty of it it's been installed by  thousands of people over many weeks and  it's still living and breathing now if  you like  for fran every heart she paints is an  act of love  for her husband steve and the many  others who were lost  it's a wall of  absolute trauma and grief and loss and  fury and rage and confusion  how has this happened in our country  that this many people  have gone they're all dead  they're all dead  and  and this this symbolizes that loss in  such a powerful way  in the years to come it may be hard to  memorialize the loss and the pain  wrought by covid  countless commemorations in countless  countries for countless dead will seek  to try and this wall made by those who  can tell the story of the pandemic best  could be a model as we all attempt to  process our collective grief  that's it for today next week we head to  portugal where the new omicron  subvariant ba-5 saw covet infections  soar despite the warm summer  temperatures  scientists are still researching the  variant which also pushed up portugal's  overall death rate we'll see you next  week  until then stay healthy  [Music]  you
 

